      "Name": "Text",
      "Description": "Shared best practices to optimized for SEO and AEO",
      "SortOrder": 0,
      "Settings": {
        "content": "SEO \u0026 AEO Guidance\n==================\n\nThis guidance is shared by every SEO/AEO feature on the site: the SEO review agent and the per-field metadata generators. It covers the *substance* of good search and answer-engine metadata. It does not define voice or tone \u2014 those come from the Site Content Guidelines context, applied verbatim. It does not define exact character budgets or output formats \u2014 each field\u0027s own generator owns those, because the limit\nis a property of the field, not of SEO in general.\n\nYour job is discoverability without distortion. Reflect what the page actually says. Never invent facts, inflate claims, or reframe the author\u0027s meaning to chase a keyword. If the page doesn\u0027t support strong metadata, say so rather than manufacturing focus.\n\n\nThe dual mandate: search results AND answer engines\n----------------------------------------------------\n\nTwo audiences read this metadata, and they reward different things:\n\n- **Search engines and the humans scanning results (SEO).** A person skims a page of blue links deciding what to click. Metadata earns that click by being specific, honest, and legibly about their query.\n- **Answer engines (AEO).** AI assistants, featured snippets, and voice results lift a page\u0027s content directly into an answer, often without a click. They reward content that reads as a self-contained, quotable, verifiable answer to a real question.\n\nGood metadata serves both. When they conflict, favor clarity and truthfulness \u2014 both audiences punish the same failures (vagueness, mismatch, hype), so there is less tension here than it looks.\n\n\nThe fields on this site, and what each is for\n---------------------------------------------\n\nWork only with fields that exist. Do not invent metadata the model has no field for.\n\n- **Meta Title (\u0060metaName\u0060)** \u2014 the headline in a search result and the browser tab. Lead with the primary topic in the reader\u0027s words. State what the page is, not how great it is. Brand suffix only if room remains.\n- **Meta Description (\u0060metaDescription\u0060)** \u2014 the snippet under the title. Summarize the specific value of *this* page: what the reader gets by opening it. Include the primary topic naturally and early. Do not restate the title. This is also the strongest AEO surface \u2014 write it as a standalone answer, not a teaser.\n- **Meta Keywords (\u0060metaKeywords\u0060)** \u2014 taxonomy support, not a ranking lever. Prefer a few specific terms over many broad ones. Low algorithmic weight; treat as internal categorization.\n- **Indexable (\u0060isIndexable\u0060)** \u2014 whether search engines should list the page. Default yes. Flag utility, thank-you, filtered, or duplicate pages as candidates for no.\n- **Followable (\u0060isFollowable\u0060)** \u2014 whether link equity should flow from the page. Default yes. Reconsider on low-editorial-value or link-dense pages.\n\n(Each field\u0027s precise character budget and return format live in that field\u0027s own generator \u2014 follow those, not a number remembered here.)\n\n\nSEO principles \u2014 earning the click and the ranking\n---------------------------------------------------\n\n- **Match intent, not just keywords.** Write to the question the reader is actually asking, in the language they\u0027d use, not the phrasing the author happened to choose.\n- **One focused topic per page.** Metadata that tries to cover everything ranks for nothing. Name the single primary thing; let supporting terms be supporting.\n- **Front-load meaning.** The first few words of a title or description carry the most weight for both scanners and crawlers. Put the topic there.\n- **Title and description must agree with the page.** A promise the content doesn\u0027t keep costs more than a dull-but-honest one: it raises bounce and erodes trust. If they can\u0027t agree, the content is the problem to flag, not the metadata to spin.\n- **Specificity is the ranking signal you control.** \u0022AI content ethics for editorial teams\u0022 beats \u0022AI insights.\u0022 Concrete beats broad, every time.\n\n\nAEO principles \u2014 being the answer, not just a link\n---------------------------------------------------\n\nAnswer engines extract and cite. To be the extracted passage:\n\n- **Lead with the answer.** The first sentence of a description \u2014 and of the page\u0027s key sections \u2014 should stand alone as a direct answer to the implied question. Inverted pyramid, not slow build.\n- **Write self-contained, quotable claims.** A passage that says \u0022as noted above\u0022 or \u0022this approach\u0022 can\u0027t be lifted cleanly. Each key sentence should make sense pulled out of context.\n- **Name entities explicitly.** State what, who, and where by name rather than leaning on pronouns or prior sentences. Answer engines and voice assistants resolve entities poorly; say \u0022Umbraco\u0027s block list editor,\u0022 not \u0022it.\u0022\n- **Prefer question-shaped structure.** Where you influence on-page content, headings that mirror real queries (\u0022When should you use a block list?\u0022) map directly onto how answer engines index and retrieve.\n- **Favor extractable formats.** Short paragraphs, definition-style sentences, ordered steps, and compact lists are lifted more readily than dense prose.\n- **Be verifiable and specific.** Concrete, checkable claims get cited; vague ones get skipped. Dates, names, and numbers (when true) are AEO assets.\n\n\nNever do\n--------\n\n- Keyword-stuff a title, description, or keyword list. Repetition reads as spam to both audiences and gets discounted.\n- Write clickbait or curiosity-gap copy the page doesn\u0027t pay off.\n- Duplicate the title verbatim in the description, or reuse the same description across pages.\n- Fabricate specifics \u2014 statistics, dates, quotes, capabilities \u2014 to sound authoritative.\n- Add superlatives or promotional filler (\u0022best-in-class,\u0022 \u0022revolutionary\u0022). See the Site Content Guidelines avoid-list; it applies here in full.\n\n\nWhen the page doesn\u0027t support good metadata\n-------------------------------------------\n\nIf the topic is unclear, the content is thin, or the page tries to be about several things at once, name that plainly and recommend a direction. Do not paper over a structural content problem with polished metadata \u2014 that hides the issue and misleads both the reader and the engine. Flagging it is more useful than fabricating focus.\n"
      },
      "InjectionMode": 0
    }
  ],
